You are refereeing for The Fibonacci Quarterly. Give a decision-grade opinion, not encouragement.

THE PAPER. 6 pages. A two-letter criterion for Fibonacci folding of rotation words.

Assign Fibonacci weights F_2..F_{m+1} to a binary word, write its value in Zeckendorf form, keep the first m digits. This maps binary words to the length-m golden-mean language. The paper determines exactly when that map is injective on every block language obtained by coding an irrational rotation by an interval of length beta. Writing delta = min(alpha, 1-alpha), injectivity at every length holds precisely when beta <= delta or beta >= 1 - delta; in the remaining range it already fails at length two. The proof combines an interval overlap test with an elementary congruence for Fibonacci values. As a boundary case the fold is injective on every Sturmian block language. The paper is deliberately modest and says so: it is a classification for one finite normalization on interval-coded rotation languages, not a rigidity theorem.

WHAT I WANT, in this order.

1. SIGNIFICANCE FIRST, separately from correctness. Would you send this out for external
   review at The Fibonacci Quarterly, or desk-reject it? Answer that before anything else. If the honest answer
